  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nRest of Today through Saturday...\n\nSurface high pressure in place over the Ohio Valley is continuing \nour stretch of dry weather across the region, with mostly sunny \nskies, light winds, and temperatures in the mid to upper 60s this \nafternoon. This stretch of calm weather will continue through the \nrest of the workweek and into the start of the weekend, though \nsteadily increasing southerly flow due to the area of surface high \npressure shifting off to the east will promote warmer temperatures. \nHighs will be in the upper 60s/near 70 on Thursday, with upper \n60s/low 70s highs Friday and Saturday. NBM probabilities for highs \nreaching 70 are in the 40-70% range south of the Parkways Thursday, \nwith the area of those probabilities covering all areas along and \nsouth of I-64 by Saturday. Overnight temperatures will still be on \nthe colder side, with mid to upper 30s lows tonight and upper \n30s/low 40s lows Friday and Saturday mornings. River valleys will be \nmore perceptible to temperatures closer to freezing tonight, \nlingering in the mid 30s Thursday and Friday night. Patchy river \nvalley fog will be possible tonight, with chances decreasing each \npassing day as we get slightly increasing southerly flow.\n\nSunday through Middle of Next Week...\n\nA shift in the synoptic pattern takes shape this weekend as a broad \nupper level trough moves towards the eastern CONUS. An associated \nsurface low and cold front will move eastward as well, with the \nfront passing over the region Sunday-early Monday. While modest \nmoisture return will allow for PW values near 0.9-1.0\" as the front \nmoves through, relatively weak forcing and the quicker progression \nof the front will keep QPF amounts below 0.25\" LREF probabilities \nfor amounts greater than 0.25\" are in the 25-35% range west of I-75, \ndecreasing to the east. \n\nSurface high pressure builds in behind the front, setting up a drier \nstart to next week. A cold air mass will drop our temperatures some \nas well, with highs in the 40s/low 50s Monday-Wednesday and lows \nnear/below freezing Tuesday and Wednesday morning. The surface high \nwill not be as persistent as our current high as the upper-level \npattern becomes more unsettled going into the middle parts of next \nweek. While there is not great agreement in the placement and timing \nin any particular system, probabilities for rainfall do increase by \nWednesday based off the most recent LREF guidance. With the given \nuncertainty, POPs remain below 30% at this time.",
